AB  - In this study, genetic diversity, correlation, principal component analysis and cluster analysis were conducted on 18 agronomic and phenotypic traits of 60 quinoa resources, in order to screen germplasm resources suitable for planting in Inner Mongolia. The results showed that this batch of quinoa materials had rich genetic diversity, and the coefficient of variation was between 13.75% to 66.74%. The correlation analysis showed that the grain weight per panicle was significantly positively correlated with plant height, stem diameter, effective panicle number, main panicle diameter, leaf length, effective branch number and main panicle length (P <0.01), significantly positively correlated with leaf width (P <0.05), and significantly negatively correlated with grain color (P <0.05). The principal component analysis showed that the cumulative contribution rate of six principal components was 72.50%, which were yield component, plant morphology and panicle color component, plant yield component, plant color component, plant type component and panicle morphology component. Cluster analysis showed that 60 quinoa materials could be divided into three groups when the genetic distance was four. Among them, three materials in group &#8546; had the characteristics of tall plant, strong stems, many effective branches, large leaf area, long main panicle, high yield and good comprehensive traits, which could further carry out genetic improvement and fine traits exploration, providing theoretical basis and information support for the protection, utilization and screening of quinoa germplasm resources. © 2022 Editorial Department of Chinese Journal of Grassland. All rights reserved.
