TI  - Genetic variation in amaranth landraces from India
AU  - Vaidya, K.R.
AU  - Jain, S.K.
T2  - Journal of Genetics and Breeding
AB  - Ten landrace populations of grain amaranths (Amaranthus hypochondriacus and A. caudatus) from two geographical regions of India were assessed for genetic variation in quantitative and qualitative characters. The quantitative characters were: leaf length, leaf width, petiole length, leaf number per plant, branch number per plant, plant height, days to flowering, inflorescence length and biomass. Significant differences for the quantitative characters were observed among these landrace populations in contrast to their largely monomorphic pattern of allozyme variation. Between - and within - family genetic variances (s2b and s2w) were estimated; between-family variances were significant for all metric characters. Evidence for heterozygosity within a large proportion of families was obtained from their segregation for qualitative characters including monogenic seedling color and digenic leaf marker traits. In fact, these landrace populations from northern India showed more intra- and inter-population variation than those sampled from the New World centers of origin. A discriminant analysis of quantitative variation further showed that the populations used in this study were genetically differentiated into at least two distinct regional groups. It appeared that an optimal germplasm collecting strategy should be based on both morphological and allozyme data: samples should include populations from many different geographical areas and from adequate numbers of individuals (e.g., 50-100 plants) per population, scored and harvested separately to provide data on heterozygote frequency as well as multigenic associations.
