TY  - JOUR
TI  - Effect of indole-3-butyric acid and gibberellic acid on rooting and growth of arracacha propagules
AU  - Rosero-Alpala, M.G.
AU  - Lozano-Fernández, J.
AU  - Velásquez-Arroyo, C.E.
AU  - Garnica Montaña, J.P.
T2  - Agronomia Colombiana
AB  - Ex situ conservation of Andean roots requires asexual propa gation to maintain germplasm genetic integrity. This study evaluated the effects of phytohormones on adventitious root formation and growth in low-rooting arracacha (Arracacia xan thorrhiza) propagules (accession CUI15010083) from Colombia Germplasm Banks for Food and Agriculture. Indole-3-butyric acid (IBA; 7.5, 15.0, 1000 or 3000 mg L-1) and gibberellic acid (GA; 50 or 100 mg L-1) were applied at varying immersion times (1, 30 min for IBA; 5 min for GA), with a water-only control. Propagation was assessed across three phases: 1) water-based rooting, 2) acclimatization in soil, and 3) field transplant, mea suring vigor, plant height, leaf length, and fresh/dry biomass. High IBA concentrations (1000-3000 mg L-1; 1 min immersion) induced propagule mortality. Lower IBA doses (&#8804;15 mg L-1; 30 min immersion) enhanced root quality, producing propagules with intermediate root category scores, though statistically comparable to controls. GA treatments (&#8804;100 mg L-1; 5 min immersion) showed no biomass differences relative to controls. Low-concentration IBA stimulated early rooting, improving ad ventitious root development, survival, and subsequent growth. GA primarily promoted cell elongation, confirming its role as a vegetative growth enhancer. © 2025, Author. All rights reserved.
